{
  "assessment": "The source provides a time-independent Hamiltonian model (Eq. 4) with two regimes: W=J (chaotic) and W=10J (prethermal MBL). The SFF K(t) is measured and compared to GOE RMT and Poisson predictions. The plateau time t_p is reported to scale exponentially with system size L in the chaotic phase but not in the MBL phase. The source provides the GOE connected SFF formula with parameters, and the fitting procedure for t_p. However, the source does not provide explicit numeric values for t_p as a function of L, nor does it provide a quantitative relation for the plateau time scaling. The only numeric control is the disorder strength W, but the source only reports two discrete values (W=J and W=10J) without a continuous domain. The readout K(t) is measured over time, but the source does not provide a continuous time domain for the SFF response; it only mentions fitting from t=48 ns. The plateau time t_p is a readout but its values are not given numerically. Therefore, the evidence cannot support a quantitative interactive experiment with a continuous control and a numeric response. case_authoring_ready is set to false.",
  "case_authoring_ready": false,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Hang Dong, Measuring the Spectral Form Factor in Many-Body Chaotic and Localized Phases of Quantum Processors, Physical Review Letters (2025), DOI:10.1103/physrevlett.134.010402",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/physrevlett.134.010402",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2403.16935v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper experimentally measures the spectral form factor (SFF) and partial SFF in superconducting quantum processors for both Floquet and time-independent Hamiltonian many-body systems, comparing chaotic and prethermal many-body localized phases. The selected cluster focuses on the time-independent Hamiltonian model (Eq. 4) with W=J (chaotic) and W=10J (prethermal MBL) regimes, and the SFF plateau time scaling with system size.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "GOE_connected_SFF",
      "statement": "The connected SFF of the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble (GOE) with N-by-N random matrices is given by a piecewise function of time t relative to the Heisenberg time t_H, with a logarithmic ramp before t_H and a different logarithmic form after t_H.",
      "equations": [
        "K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2t/t_H - (t/t_H) ln(1 + 2t/t_H)] for t < t_H",
        "K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2 - (t/t_H) ln((2t/t_H + 1)/(2t/t_H - 1))] for t > t_H"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["K_GOE", "t", "t_H", "N"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "GOE_ramp_coefficient",
          "value": 2,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient in the ramp term 2t/t_H",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 342,
              "line_end": 342,
              "support": "The connected SFF of the GOE is given by K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2t/t_H - (t/t_H) ln(1+2t/t_H)] before t_H, and K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2 - (t/t_H) ln((2t/t_H+1)/(2t/t_H-1))] after t_H."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "GOE_log_coefficient",
          "value": 1,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "coefficient multiplying the logarithmic terms",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 342,
              "line_end": 342,
              "support": "The logarithmic terms are multiplied by 1 in both expressions."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 342,
          "line_end": 342,
          "support": "The connected SFF of the GOE with N-by-N random matrices is given by K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2t/t_H - (t/t_H) ln(1+2t/t_H)] before t_H, and K_GOE(t) = (1/N)[2 - (t/t_H) ln((2t/t_H+1)/(2t/t_H-1))] after t_H."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "plateau_time_scaling",
      "statement": "The plateau time t_p of the SFF increases exponentially with system size L in the chaotic phase (W=J), but does not increase exponentially in the prethermal MBL phase (W=10J).",
      "equations": [
        "t_p ~ exponential in L for W=J",
        "t_p does not scale exponentially with L for W=10J"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["t_p", "L"],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 347,
          "line_end": 349,
          "support": "For W=J, SFF displays a dip, ramp, and plateau with t_p scaling exponentially in the system size. For W=10J, t_p does not increase exponentially with system size."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "hamiltonian_model_parameters",
      "statement": "The time-independent Hamiltonian is H = H_XY + h^x sum_m sigma_m^x + sum_m h^z_m sigma_m^z, with coupling strength J = -5 MHz and transverse field h^x = 2 MHz. The local potentials h^z_m are randomly sampled from a uniform distribution in [-W, W].",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 323,
          "line_end": 327,
          "support": "The Hamiltonian is defined with J = -5 MHz and h^x = 2 MHz, and h^z_m sampled uniformly from [-W, W]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "chaotic_regime_W_eq_J",
      "statement": "The chaotic regime is realized with disorder strength W = J, where J = -5 MHz, so W = -5 MHz (magnitude 5 MHz).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 333,
          "line_end": 333,
          "support": "The Hamiltonian system is simulated in the chaotic phase with W=J."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "prethermal_MBL_regime_W_eq_10J",
      "statement": "The prethermal MBL regime is realized with disorder strength W = 10J, where J = -5 MHz, so W = -50 MHz (magnitude 50 MHz).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 333,
          "line_end": 333,
          "support": "The Hamiltonian system is simulated in the prethermal MBL phase with W=10J."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "system_sizes_up_to_L5",
      "statement": "The experiments are performed for system sizes L up to 5, with 400 to 1000 realizations.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 333,
          "line_end": 333,
          "support": "The Hamiltonian system is simulated in the chaotic phase with W=J and prethermal MBL phase with W=10J for 400 to 1000 realizations up to the system size L=5."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "fitting_start_time_48ns",
      "statement": "The SFF data is fitted starting from t = 48 ns, when the SFF has reached its lowest value.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 336,
          "line_end": 336,
          "support": "For each inset, we numerically fit the error-mitigated and normalized SFF data K'(t) with the GOE RMT behavior, for the domain in the shaded region of the main panel starting from t=48 ns when the SFF has reached its lowest value."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "disorder_strength_W",
      "definition": "The disorder strength W determines the amplitude of the random local potentials h^z_m, which are sampled uniformly from [-W, W]. The value of W determines whether the system is in the chaotic (W=J) or prethermal MBL (W=10J) regime.",
      "unit": "MHz",
      "minimum": -50,
      "maximum": -5,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 327,
          "line_end": 327,
          "support": "The local potentials h^z_m are randomly and independently sampled from a uniform distribution within the range [-W, W]. The value of W determines the dynamical regime of the system."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 333,
          "line_end": 333,
          "support": "The Hamiltonian system is simulated in the chaotic phase with W=J and prethermal MBL phase with W=10J."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "spectral_form_factor_K",
      "definition": "The spectral form factor K(t) is defined as K(t) = (1/N^2) overline{|Tr U(t)|^2}, where U(t) is the time evolution operator. It is normalized such that K(0)=1.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "ensemble average over statistically-similar systems",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 222,
          "line_end": 233,
          "support": "The SFF is defined as K(t) = (1/N^2) overline{|Tr U(t)|^2}, with normalization K(0)=1."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "plateau_time_t_p",
      "definition": "The plateau time t_p is defined as the time when the SFF reaches its plateau value.",
      "unit": "ns",
      "statistic": "determined from fits to error-mitigated SFF data",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "resolution": null,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 345,
          "line_end": 345,
          "support": "For both cases, for sufficiently large time, SFF will reach the plateau at the plateau time t_p."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 408,
          "line_end": 409,
          "support": "To extract the plateau times of the SFF for the Hamiltonian model, we numerically fit the error-mitigated data K_em^(1)(t) using the formula f(t; mu, t_H) = mu K_GOE(t; t_H), where mu and t_H are fitting parameters."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "chaotic_phase_GOE_fit",
      "statement": "For W=J, the error-mitigated SFF data K'(t) is fitted with the GOE RMT behavior, and the SFF displays a dip-ramp-plateau behavior with plateau time scaling exponentially with system size.",
      "quantitative_form": "K'(t) = mu^{-1} N K_em^(1)(t) fitted to N K_GOE(t; t_H) for t >= 48 ns",
      "mechanism_ids": ["GOE_connected_SFF", "plateau_time_scaling"],
      "control_names": ["disorder_strength_W"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "disorder_strength_W",
          "minimum": -5,
          "maximum": -5,
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 333,
              "line_end": 333,
              "support": "The chaotic phase is simulated with W=J."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["spectral_form_factor_K", "plateau_time_t_p"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["hamiltonian_model_parameters", "chaotic_regime_W_eq_J", "system_sizes_up_to_L5", "fitting_start_time_48ns"],
      "applicability": "Applies to the time-independent Hamiltonian model with W=J, for system sizes up to L=5, and for times after the initial dip (t >= 48 ns).",
      "statistical_basis": "Experimental data from 400 to 1000 realizations, error-mitigated and fitted to GOE RMT.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 336,
          "line_end": 336,
          "support": "For each inset, we numerically fit the error-mitigated and normalized SFF data K'(t) with the GOE RMT behavior, for the domain in the shaded region of the main panel starting from t=48 ns."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 347,
          "line_end": 347,
          "support": "For W=J, SFF displays an initial dip, a ramp, and a plateau with t_p scaling exponentially in the system size."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "prethermal_MBL_phase_poisson",
      "statement": "For W=10J, the SFF dips and then plateaus relatively quickly, with plateau time not increasing exponentially with system size, consistent with a prethermal MBL phase.",
      "quantitative_form": "t_p does not scale exponentially with L",
      "mechanism_ids": ["plateau_time_scaling"],
      "control_names": ["disorder_strength_W"],
      "control_domains": [
        {
          "control_name": "disorder_strength_W",
          "minimum": -50,
          "maximum": -50,
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 333,
              "line_end": 333,
              "support": "The prethermal MBL phase is simulated with W=10J."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "readout_names": ["spectral_form_factor_K", "plateau_time_t_p"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["hamiltonian_model_parameters", "prethermal_MBL_regime_W_eq_10J", "system_sizes_up_to_L5"],
      "applicability": "Applies to the time-independent Hamiltonian model with W=10J, for system sizes up to L=5.",
      "statistical_basis": "Experimental data from 400 to 1000 realizations, compared to Poissonian statistics.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 349,
          "line_end": 349,
          "support": "For W=10J, the SFF dips and then plateaus relatively quickly, at times much earlier than the case of W=J. Crucially, unlike the chaotic case, t_p does not increase exponentially with system size."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_ambiguities": [
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "W_sign_convention",
      "statement": "The disorder strength W is defined as the upper bound of the uniform distribution [-W, W]. Since J is negative (-5 MHz), W=J gives W=-5 MHz and W=10J gives W=-50 MHz. The sign of W is not explicitly discussed; the magnitude is what matters for the disorder strength.",
      "claim_ids": ["chaotic_phase_GOE_fit", "prethermal_MBL_phase_poisson"],
      "mechanism_ids": ["plateau_time_scaling"],
      "control_names": ["disorder_strength_W"],
      "readout_names": ["spectral_form_factor_K", "plateau_time_t_p"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chaotic_regime_W_eq_J", "prethermal_MBL_regime_W_eq_10J"],
      "anchors": [
